---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const labels = [
  { id: 'temperature-ratio', tex: String.raw`T/S` },
  { id: 'spin-energy', tex: String.raw`Sk^2` },
  { id: 'bloch-power', tex: String.raw`T^{3/2}` },
  { id: 'bloch-coefficient', tex: String.raw`\frac{\zeta(3/2)}{8\pi^{3/2}}` },
  { id: 'claim-leading', tex: String.raw`\beta^{3/2}(S-m_{S,J}(\beta))\longrightarrow B_{S,J}` },
  { id: 'claim-limit', tex: String.raw`B_{S,J}=\frac{\zeta(3/2)}{8\pi^{3/2}\sqrt{\det D_{S,J}}}` },
] as const;
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-tilted-waves"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  step={50}
  labels={labels}
  description="Sixteen upright schematic arrows, ten on phones, tilt into a long wave. A second row illustrates a shorter wave. On positive logarithmic temperature axes, a dashed Bloch asymptote draws before a solid numerical approximation to the ideal nearest-neighbour magnon-density integral. Absolute zero is marked separately outside the logarithmic axis. Three ideal-only ratios compare the integral with the dashed asymptote. The original arrows carry into a five-by-five grid, four-by-four on phones, explicitly labelled a schematic slice of a three-dimensional quantum system. No arrow is a definite quantum-spin direction. A blue bracket marks only the claimed low-temperature limit, while ink formulas show its dispersion-dependent coefficient and the ideal integral's next-term ratio. The closing ledger preserves the dated unreviewed status and distinguishes the linked, uncatalogued magnetization statement from the three results without a release statement."
  caption="Solid ink is a numerical approximation to the ideal nearest-neighbour magnon-density integral; dashed ink is Bloch's asymptote. Neither is a finite-temperature curve for the interacting quantum magnet. The arrows are schematic, not a classical simulation or definite quantum directions. The blue bracket identifies the paper's limiting claim in three dimensions for every fixed quantum spin and every symmetric nonnegative finite-range coupling whose support generates the cubic lattice. Its coefficient depends on the quadratic dispersion matrix D. The limits are volume, then field, then temperature; no numerical temperature threshold is supplied."
  leftOut="The loop representation and proof; quantum-spin measurements; a classical simulation; any finite-temperature quantum magnetization curve or critical temperature; numerical threshold bounds; higher corrections beyond the first ideal lattice term; the one- and two-dimensional cases."
>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The arrow row and grid are schematic. They are not sampled from a classical Gibbs model and do not assign definite directions to quantum spins. The wave tilt is a drawing convention guided by the ideal-density cursor, not a measured magnetization. The same arrows move from the row into a grid, with further arrows introduced to complete it. The grid is a slice or projection of a three-dimensional system, not a two-dimensional ferromagnet. The short-wave row illustrates a higher energy cost without simulating dynamics.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The only computed density is the nearest-neighbour ideal-magnon integral <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\delta(\beta S)=\int_{[-\pi,\pi]^3}\frac{d^3k}{(2\pi)^3}\frac1{e^{2\beta S\sum_j(1-\cos k_j)}-1}`, false)} />. The browser evaluates a numerical approximation to this exact mathematical integral. It is not a calculation of the interacting quantum model at any finite temperature. The plotted solid line and all finite numerical readouts refer only to this ideal gas. The dimensionless horizontal parameter is <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`T/S`, false)} />, with <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\beta=1/T`, false)} />. Absolute zero is outside the positive logarithmic axis and has a separate mark with a break.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The dashed line is Bloch's asymptote <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\frac{\zeta(3/2)}{8\pi^{3/2}}(\beta S)^{-3/2}`, false)} />. The textbook and experimental-confirmation tag attaches to this dashed law alone. The solid integral is compared with it at <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`T/S=1/2,\ 1/8,\ 1/128`, false)} />. These ideal-only ratios approach one as the parameter decreases. The later next-term ratio also concerns only the ideal integral: it divides the difference between that integral and Bloch's term by <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`3\zeta(5/2)(\beta S)^{-5/2}/(128\pi^{3/2})`, false)} />. It does not test the papers' interacting-quantum correction claim.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The general Bloch claim concerns every fixed quantum spin <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`S\in\{1/2,1,3/2,\ldots\}`, false)} /> and every symmetric nonnegative finite-range coupling <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`J`, false)} /> on <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\mathbb Z^3`, false)} /> whose support generates the lattice. The matrix <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`D_{S,J}`, false)} /> is the quadratic coefficient of the one-magnon dispersion. The claimed coefficient is <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\zeta(3/2)/(8\pi^{3/2}\sqrt{\det D_{S,J}})`, false)} />. For the nearest-neighbour example <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`D_{S,J}=SI`, false)} />, giving the displayed ideal-gas normalization. A schematic nearest-neighbour grid does not narrow the general Bloch claim to nearest neighbours.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The order of limits is part of the claim: first the volume tends to infinity, then the positive field tends to zero, then temperature tends to zero. The bracket marks that limiting statement, not a region of verified finite-temperature quantum values or a computed threshold. The other three papers have narrower hypotheses: the magnetization theorem uses nearest neighbours in dimensions at least three; the correction and spherical-direction papers use the nearest-neighbour three-dimensional model. The film does not merge their scope with the more general finite-range Bloch theorem.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The closing ledger preserves the article's unreviewed status and evidence date, 3 November 2026. The pinned release's scope note links <code>Heisenberg.lean</code>, which states the magnetization theorem alone and is not catalogued. None of the four papers is in that release's formalization catalogue. Bloch's law, its correction and the spherical law have no comparator statement there. The historical search found no outside replay, and this film performs no release-proof replay. The presence of a statement or a kernel check does not count as reading or approving the papers.</p>
  <p class="film-method">With JavaScript disabled, the article and transcript remain available without a fallback illustration. Reduced motion selects a completed supporting frame for each of the nine exact canonical cues.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
